],argument:[
s("S001","Fix t0>0 and 1≤j≤n. Choose C,R from N002 and put Q=[−R,R]^n. At t0, g_j, its jth derivative and the two summands B_j,T_j are continuous, integrable, and zero outside C⊂int Q. N003 gives smoothness and the pointwise identity ∂_{x_j}g_j=B_j+T_j.",["N002","N003"],[],[...gg,"t0","j","C","R","Q"],[f("F001","At t0 the functions g_j, ∂_{x_j}g_j, B_j,T_j are integrable and vanish off C⊂int Q; ∂_{x_j}g_j=B_j+T_j.",[...gg,"t0","j","C","Q"])]),
s("S002","Define Xhat to be the tuple space of all coordinates except j with product Lebesgue measure, and χ(x)=((x_i)_{i≠j},x_j). Apply M011 with predicate i≠j to split the finite product measure. The remaining block {j} has a unique element; M017 identifies its product measure with ordinary one-dimensional Lebesgue measure by evaluation at j. Composing these explicit measurable equivalences shows that χ is a measure-preserving measurable equivalence from V to Xhat×R. The inverse is coordinate insertion. When n=1, Xhat is the one-point empty product with mass one, so the same identification applies.",["M011","M017"],[],[...gg,"t0","j","R","Q","Xhat","χ"],[f("F002","χ:V→Xhat×R and its inverse are measurable, χ is bijective and preserves dx and the product of the Lebesgue measures on Xhat and R.",["χ","V","Xhat"])]),
s("S003","Fix any y∈Xhat and define G(z)=g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0). By N003 this function is smooth and G'(z)=∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0). If z=±R, the inserted point lies outside C, since C⊂(−R,R)^n; hence G(−R)=G(R)=0. The derivative is continuous on the finite interval [−R,R], so it is interval integrable there. M006 gives ∫_{−R}^R G'(z)dz=G(R)−G(−R)=0. This is also the integral on the closed interval: its endpoints have Lebesgue measure zero. For |z|>R the derivative vanishes by F001; thus ∫_R ∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0) dz=0. This proves the zero inner integral for every y, including when some other coordinate lies outside the box.",["M006","N003"],["F001","F002"],[...gg,"t0","j","C","R","Q","Xhat","χ","y","G"],[f("F003","For every y∈Xhat, the function z↦∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0) is integrable on R and its integral over R, equivalently over [−R,R], is zero.",["g","j","χ","t0","R","Xhat"],[b("y","point of Xhat"),b("z","real")])]),
s("S004","Define H(y,z)=∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0). It is continuous and supported in the compact product box corresponding to Q, hence integrable; equivalently integrability follows by transport through the measure-preserving measurable equivalence in F002 from F001. Apply M016 with the coordinate equivalence χ and scalar function H: ∫_V ∂_{x_j}g_j(x,t0)dx=∫_{Xhat×R}H. Apply M007 to this integrable H. The product Lebesgue factors are sigma-finite, hence s-finite, including the empty factor. F003 makes each inner integral zero. Therefore ∫_V ∂_{x_j}g_j(x,t0)dx=∫_{Xhat}0=0. Restricting to Q gives the identical box integral, since its integrand vanishes off Q. This is precisely the coordinate FTC/Fubini integration-by-parts step.",["M016","M007"],["F001","F002","F003"],[...gg,"t0","j","R","Q","Xhat","χ","H"],[f("F004","∫_V ∂_{x_j}g_j(x,t0) dx=0.",["V","g","j","t0"],[b("x","point of V")])]),
s("S005","Integrate the identity in F001 and use M013 with its two integrable summands B_j(·,t0),T_j(·,t0). F004 yields 0=∫_V B_j+∫_V T_j. Rearranging gives ∫_V B_j=−∫_V T_j. Unfold the definitions of B and T to get exactly the asserted vector-dot-product formula. Generalize over t0>0 and all spatial indices j.",["M013"],["F001","F004"],[...gg,"t0","j"])
],applications:[
app("A001","M011","S002","Set I={1,...,n}, each X_i=R with Lebesgue μ_i, and p(i)=(i≠j).",["The coordinate index set is finite and each real line has its Lebesgue measurable structure.","Lebesgue measure on R is sigma-finite (covered by the finite-measure intervals [−k,k]).","Equality of finite coordinate indices is decidable."]),
app("A002","M017","S002","Use the singleton coordinate type {i:1≤i≤n,i=j}, X_i=R, μ_i=one-dimensional Lebesgue measure; evaluate at j.",["Because 1≤j≤n this finite subtype has exactly one element, j; all spaces and measures are the real Lebesgue ones."]),
